A warehouse robot shelves identical boxes into a bay and always fills complete rows before starting a new one. Its controller reports three facts about the total number N of boxes currently in the bay:

- when arranged 4 boxes per row, the final incomplete row holds 3 boxes;
- when arranged 6 boxes per row, the final incomplete row holds 5 boxes;
- when arranged 9 boxes per row, the final incomplete row holds 8 boxes.

The bay can hold fewer than 1000 boxes. How many positive integer values of N are consistent with all three reports?

Consider the recursive function below (indentation shows the body):

  function f(n):
      if n <= 1:
          return n
      return f(n - 1) + f(n - 2)

Each call either returns immediately (when n <= 1) or makes two further calls. During a single top-level evaluation of f(5), how many of the calls return immediately because they reach the base case n <= 1?

In the pseudocode below, both loop ranges are inclusive of their endpoints, and n = 10:

  for i = 1 to n:
      for j = i to n:
          if (i + j) is even:
              print(i, j)

That is, the outer variable i runs 1, 2, ..., n; for each i the inner variable j runs i, i+1, ..., n; and the pair is printed only when i + j is even.

How many times is the print statement executed in total?
